# direnv cached dev shell
.direnv/

# Build caches and outputs — large, regenerable, never commit.

# arduino-cli isolated data dir, package downloads, and per-user dir
build/data/
build/downloads/
build/user/
# arduino-cli build output (.bin / .elf / .map / objects)
build/output/
# compiled host unit-test binaries
test/output/

# Arduino IDE build output sitting INSIDE the sketch folder. Inherited from the
# vendor drops (v1.2.1, v1.2.3), referenced by nothing, and superseded by
# build/output/ which the Makefile writes. It was tracked, and the patterns
# above did not cover it: a gitignore pattern containing a slash is anchored to
# the repo root, so `build/output/` never matched this path.
#
# **It cost 17.8 MB on every `nix develop`.** Nix copies the working tree into
# the store to evaluate a flake from a DIRTY git tree, so each distinct tree
# state during a session is a fresh copy. Measured 2026-08-13: ~230 MB per two
# evaluations, which is how the root filesystem reached 100% with 233 GB of
# /nix/store.
#
# **It copies TRACKED files only** -- an earlier note here claimed untracking
# alone would not help and that the ignore entry is what stops the copy. That is
# refuted. Measured the same day with a control: a 5 MB untracked, *unignored*
# file at the repo root, with a tracked file touched to force a fresh evaluation
# so the result could not be a cache hit. A new store path was created and the
# file was absent from it.
#
# So untracking is what stops the copy and these entries are hygiene -- they
# keep `git status` readable and stop an accidental `git add -A`. Worth having
# for that alone, but do not credit them with the disk saving.
GBSC-Pro-Source code/gbs-control/build/

# Python bytecode (test_loopback.py imports the flasher module)
__pycache__/

# Editor and agent settings, which are one person's machine rather than the
# project: .claude/settings.json holds absolute paths under /home/msharman.
.claude/

# web UI toolchain: `npm ci` in GBSC-Pro-Source code/gbs-control/public installs
# the tsc the repo pins (4.1.3), which the build needs and nixpkgs cannot supply
node_modules/

# Bench photographs and video -- 67 files, 146 MB -- now in their own repo:
#
#     https://github.com/TheCodeSharman/gbsc-pro-bench-photos
#
# Not a preference. This is a PUBLIC FORK of RetroScaler/gbsc-pro, LFS objects
# on a fork bill to the parent's storage, and GitHub refuses them outright:
# "can not upload new objects to public fork". Converting this repo's history to
# LFS is therefore unavailable however it is scoped -- and `git lfs migrate`
# rewrites from the root commit regardless, which would move upstream's SHAs.
#
# Untracking them cut the nix evaluation copy from 211 MB to 65 MB, measured
# 2026-08-13. That is the largest single saving in the whole exercise, and LFS
# could never have delivered it: LFS smudges the images back into the working
# tree and they stay tracked, so the copy would still have been 211 MB.
#
# The photo repo mirrors these paths exactly, so its tree drops straight back
# into a checkout and lands right here, ignored. WHAT EACH PHOTOGRAPH SHOWS
# stays in this repo -- the tables in docs/photos/*/README.md and
# snapshots/LOG.md are the part that is read and the part that diffs.
docs/photos/**/*.jpg
docs/photos/**/*.jpeg
docs/photos/**/*.png
docs/photos/**/*.mov
tools/gbsc-pro-hwtest/snapshots/**/*.jpg
tools/gbsc-pro-hwtest/snapshots/**/*.jpeg
tools/gbsc-pro-hwtest/snapshots/**/*.png
tools/gbsc-pro-hwtest/snapshots/**/*.mov
